  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving the existing social security systems - bridging gaps of inequity and expanding coverage of social protection/ social security to the informal sector</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Thailand has a comprehensive social protection system, combining a tax-financed Old Age Allowance for persons aged 60 and above with contributory social insurance for formal workers under the Social Security Act, administered by the Social Security Office (SSO). Key challenges include benefit adequacy, financial sustainability amid rapid ageing, and coverage gaps in the informal economy. The ILO supports strengthening the SSO&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x2122;s capacity for evidence-based reform, with the following outcomes this biennium:

1. Strengthened actuarial capacity within the SSO, including institutional arrangements and trained staff to deliver in-house actuarial services.
2. Evidence-based policy reforms informed by actuarial valuations and technical studies to improve sustainability and expand coverage.
3. Enhanced advocacy and communication on social security reform, including capacity-building for social partners and informed public dialogue.
4. Effectively and strategically use of the Review of the social security system against ILO standards, including Convention No. 102, to guide reforms and alignment with international benchmarks.

Sustained impacts include stronger institutional capacity, improved sustainability, expanded coverage to underserved groups (including migrant, platform and domestic workers), and closer alignment with international standards.</narrative>
  </description>
